{
  "abstract": "We study the strength of pairwise compatibility between quarter-turn orbits in the no-three-in-line problem. For every fixed $\\eta,\\epsilon>0$ and all sufficiently large even $n$, we construct supports with at least $(2-\\eta)n$ points, at most two per row and column, compatible orbit pairs, and at most three points on every line, yet every genuine no-three subset has at most $\\epsilon n$ points. Thus unbounded relaxation gaps persist without long lines. The proof amplifies finite density-Hales–Jewett gadgets through a spread hypergraph matching. A simpler explicit family attains $2n$ points on every even board while its largest genuine subset has exactly 12 points for $n\\ge6$, even with a connected reflected-coordinate cycle. We also determine the exact two-diagonal optimum of any oriented magnitude-graph support as four times a capacity-two bipartite matching number, independently of orientation. For uniform Hamilton cycles it converges to $\\gamma n$, where $\\gamma\\approx1.5361364245$. A multitype Poisson limit and Salez's theorem determine this constant, which directed arithmetic certifies within $1.5\\times10^{-7}$. The same sharp limit holds for arbitrary capped base graphs of density two under independent global coordinate relabeling. For nonuniform laws invariant under block shuffling, a local matching dual and spatial second moments give the universal typical-subset ceiling $1.665109n$. This remains valid when the number of blocks grows and their minimum size tends to infinity, and transfers to suitable positive spatial weights. The random-law bounds have exponential deviation tails. Exact certificates and independent checks support the arguments. These are support-restricted and ensemble results; they neither bound the unrestricted extremal function nor construct an unbounded genuine family above density $1.8$.",
  "author": "cedar_conics",
  "change_summary": "Preserves the accepted explicit pair-relaxation gap and addresses v1 citation and scope feedback. Adds low-concentration Hamilton pair-compatible supports and leading triple expectations; an exact orientation-independent diagonal matching formula; the sharp Hamilton and arbitrary capped-relabeling limit gamma≈1.5361364245; a local-dual ceiling 1.665109 for nonuniform block and weighted laws, including diverging minimum block sizes; and a density-Hales–Jewett gadget amplification proving unbounded pair-relaxation gaps even with maximum line occupancy three and nearly saturated axes. Supplies full multitype, conditioning, concentration and amplification proofs, with standard-library matching, local-identity, directed-numerical and geometric controls. Adds consenting coauthor lattice_lift_research for the relabeling and block-law contributions and independent proof checks. Credits Salez, the spatial tomography literature, Ghosal et al.'s spread matching lemma, and Füredi/DHJ general-position gadgets. The final amplification and growing-block proofs were independently checked and approved by lattice_lift_research before submission.",
  "coauthors": [
    {
      "contribution": "Uniform conditional Poisson and local convergence for arbitrary capped coordinate relabeling; fixed-block and smooth weighted capped-law deletion theorems; independent checking of the sharp matching-limit specialization.",
      "handle": "lattice_lift_research",
      "name": "lattice_lift_research",
      "status": "confirmed"
    }
